Open Photon README fresh-clone verification, 2026-10-21
Tested remote revision: cd46be1f6d0d411036c0d47c41932cedb541bc25
Result: PASS twice, including two real Comfy jobs and browser compare playback.

Scope: two independent GitHub clones in new temporary directories on the existing
Apple Silicon host, with Colima and Chrome already installed. This was not a new
machine, VM, or user account. Docker base layers were available, the first run
executed npm ci and the production build, and the repeat reused build layers.
Neither clone inherited node_modules, data/, or .env. Each Compose project created
a different, empty sqlite-data volume. No rows or finished renders were imported.
The README's linux/amd64 platform was kept and ran emulated on Apple Silicon.

README commands executed in each clone:
$ git clone https://github.com/WojciechJelen/open-photon.git <new-temp>/open-photon
$ cd <new-temp>/open-photon
$ cp .env.example .env
Set only COMFY_API_KEY from the existing authorized key, without logging its value.
$ docker compose up --build
In a separate terminal after startup:
$ docker compose exec app node scripts/mint-code.mjs me 20
[generated access code withheld]

Host setup: DOCKER_CONTEXT=colima, as the README documents. Unique
COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME values isolated the test volumes from the existing app.
The existing container was stopped to free port 3030; its volume was kept.

Browser flow, repeated against each fresh database at http://localhost:3030:
1. Open studio, Start with Genjutsu. Select Street dance, Continue, Placeholder blue.
2. Change motion, upload driving-12s.mp4. The access dialog keeps the waiting file.
3. Enter the freshly minted code. Upload resumes and the trim dialog opens.
4. Move Trim end from 8 to 5 seconds and confirm Use these 5 seconds.
5. Continue, upload fixtures/wan/reference.png. Use photo is disabled without consent.
6. Check consent, Use photo, Continue. Keep the default angle, prompt, and seed.
7. Generate submits a real cloud job. Wait for Your shot is ready, play both videos,
   download the MP4, and inspect its video and audio streams with ffprobe.

The 12-second upload fixture was made by repeating the cloned repo's own tracked
fixtures/wan/driving.mp4 using ffmpeg -stream_loop 2 -t 12 -c copy. No private media
was used. The image was the tracked workflow reference fixture.
Samples were selected before testing uploads; the paid renders used the uploaded
cut and consented image. This does not verify a free pre-rendered sample matrix.

Application/README setup workarounds: none. No application files were changed.
Browser automation note: the DevTools connector had a busy browser profile. The
walkthrough used the connected Chrome extension instead. Clicking the hidden photo
file input timed out in the first run; clicking its visible label worked normally.
The second run used visible upload labels throughout without picker timeouts.
One console error came from an unrelated chrome-extension:// companion script;
no application-origin console errors were observed.

